__registerContent("gs1/geography/ncert/class7/ch2/ch2", {"notes":{"geo7-ch2":{"era":"NCERT Class 7 — Geography (Our Environment), Chapter 2","significance":["Interior of the Earth, Sial/Sima/Nife nomenclature, and rock types are individually high-frequency Prelims static facts","Extrusive vs intrusive igneous rocks with examples (basalt/granite) are classic MCQ traps","Rock cycle, fossil definition, and monument rock types (Red Fort = red sandstone, Taj Mahal = white marble) are frequently tested"],"features":[{"key":"Interior of the Earth — Structure","main":"Earth is made up of several concentric layers (like an onion)\n3 main layers from surface to centre: Crust → Mantle → Core","note":""},{"key":"Layer 1: Crust","main":"Uppermost layer over the Earth's surface\nThinnest of all layers\n~35 km thick on continental masses; only ~5 km thick on ocean floors\nMain mineral constituents of continental mass: silica + alumina → called SIAL (si-silica, al-alumina)\nOceanic crust consists of silica + magnesium → called SIMA (si-silica, ma-magnesium)","note":"Crust forms only 1% of the volume of the Earth"},{"key":"Layer 2: Mantle","main":"Just beneath the crust; extends to a depth of 2,900 km below the crust","note":"Mantle = 84% of the volume of the Earth"},{"key":"Layer 3: Core","main":"Innermost layer; radius of about 3,500 km\nMainly made up of nickel and iron → called NIFE (ni-nickel, fe-ferrous/iron)\nHas very high temperature and pressure","note":"Core = 15% of the volume of the Earth; Radius of the Earth = 6,371 km"},{"key":"Volume Distribution (% of Earth)","main":"Crust: 1%; Mantle: 84%; Core: 15%","note":""},{"key":"Rocks and Minerals — Definitions","main":"Any natural mass of mineral matter making up the Earth's crust = a rock\nRocks can be of different colour, size and texture\nMinerals: naturally occurring substances with certain physical properties and definite chemical composition","note":"Gold, petroleum, coal are examples of minerals"},{"key":"Three Types of Rocks","main":"1. Igneous rocks (primary rocks)\n2. Sedimentary rocks\n3. Metamorphic rocks","note":"Word origins: Igneous = Latin 'Ignis' (fire); Sedimentary = Latin 'sedimentum' (settle down); Metamorphic = Greek 'metamorphose' (change of form)"},{"key":"Igneous Rocks","main":"Formed when molten magma cools and becomes solid\nAlso called primary rocks\nTwo types:\n(a) Extrusive igneous rocks: lava cools rapidly on Earth's surface → very fine grained; Example: BASALT (Deccan Plateau is made of basalt)\n(b) Intrusive igneous rocks: magma cools slowly deep inside the crust → large grains; Example: GRANITE (grinding stones used for spices are made of granite)","note":""},{"key":"Sedimentary Rocks","main":"Rocks broken down into small fragments called sediments\nSediments transported and deposited by wind, water, etc.\nLoose sediments are compressed and hardened to form layers of rock = sedimentary rocks\nExample: sandstone (made from grains of sand)\nMay contain fossils of plants, animals and other micro-organisms","note":"Fossils: remains of dead plants and animals trapped in the layers of rocks"},{"key":"Metamorphic Rocks","main":"Igneous and sedimentary rocks can change into metamorphic rocks under great heat and pressure\nExample: clay changes into SLATE; limestone changes into MARBLE","note":"Taj Mahal = white marble; Red Fort = red sandstone"},{"key":"Rock Cycle","main":"One type of rock changes to another in a cyclic manner\nProcess: Magma cools → Igneous rock → breaks down → Sediments → compressed → Sedimentary rock → heat and pressure → Metamorphic rock → melts under heat/pressure → Magma again\nThis cyclic transformation is called the rock cycle","note":""},{"key":"Uses of Rocks","main":"Hard rocks used for making roads, houses and buildings\nPowder/paste for spices prepared with granite grinding stones\nUsed in many traditional games (seven stones, hopscotch, five stones)","note":""},{"key":"Minerals — Importance","main":"Minerals are very important to humankind\nUsed as fuels: coal, natural gas, petroleum\nUsed in industries: iron, aluminium, gold, uranium\nUsed in medicine and fertilisers","note":""}],"traps":[{"label":"Sial/Sima/Nife — Mnemonics","lines":["SIAL = Si (silica) + Al (alumina) → continental crust","SIMA = Si (silica) + Ma (magnesium) → oceanic crust","NIFE = Ni (nickel) + Fe (ferrous/iron) → core","Common trap: swapping Sial/Sima or misattributing to the wrong layer"]},{"label":"Extrusive vs Intrusive Igneous Rocks","lines":["EXTRUSIVE: lava cools RAPIDLY on SURFACE → FINE grained; Example: BASALT","INTRUSIVE: magma cools SLOWLY DEEP inside crust → LARGE grains; Example: GRANITE","Easy memory: EXtrusive = EXposed = fine; INtrusive = INside = large grains"]},{"label":"Volume % of Earth's Layers","lines":["Crust = only 1%","Mantle = 84% (biggest by volume)","Core = 15%","Crust is thinnest but has all surface life"]},{"label":"Monument Rock Types","lines":["Red Fort = RED SANDSTONE (sedimentary rock)","Taj Mahal = WHITE MARBLE (metamorphic rock)","Both are classic UPSC Prelims application questions"]},{"label":"Rock Cycle Direction","lines":["Magma → (cools) → Igneous → (weathers/erodes) → Sediments → (compressed) → Sedimentary → (heat+pressure) → Metamorphic → (melts) → Magma","The cycle is continuous — no fixed starting point"]},{"label":"Deccan Plateau Rock Type","lines":["Deccan Plateau = BASALT (extrusive igneous rock)","Basalt has very fine grained structure","NOT granite — granite is intrusive"]}],"mainsAngle":"The rock cycle illustrates how the Earth's materials are continuously recycled — a natural process undermined by unsustainable mining. The economic importance of minerals (fuels, industrial metals, medicines) directly connects to GS-3 topics on resource management, mineral security, and India's mining policy.","mainsTheme":"Earth's interior and rock cycle — foundation for understanding mineral resources and land-use policy","recall":["3 layers: Crust → Mantle → Core","Crust: 1% (thinnest); Mantle: 84%; Core: 15% (nife, 3500km radius)","Continental crust = SIAL; Oceanic crust = SIMA; Core = NIFE","Earth's radius = 6,371 km; Mantle extends 2,900 km below crust","3 rock types: Igneous (primary), Sedimentary, Metamorphic","Extrusive (basalt, fine grained) vs Intrusive (granite, coarse grained)","Deccan Plateau = basalt; Red Fort = sandstone; Taj Mahal = marble","Sedimentary rocks may contain fossils","Clay → slate (metamorphic); Limestone → marble (metamorphic)","Rock cycle: Magma → Igneous → Sedimentary → Metamorphic → Magma"]}},"quizzes":{"geo7-ch2":{"title":"Inside Our Earth","questions":[{"q":"The Earth is made up of several concentric layers. Which of the following correctly lists them from outermost to innermost?","options":["Core → Mantle → Crust","Crust → Mantle → Core","Mantle → Crust → Core","Crust → Core → Mantle"],"correct":1,"explain":"From outermost to innermost: Crust → Mantle → Core. The Crust is at the surface, Core is at the centre."},{"q":"Which layer of the Earth is called the THINNEST of all layers?","options":["Core","Inner Core","Mantle","Crust"],"correct":3,"explain":"The Crust is the thinnest of all layers — about 35 km on continental masses and only 5 km on ocean floors."},{"q":"The continental crust is called 'SIAL' because:","options":["It contains silicon and aluminium as main mineral constituents","It contains silicon and magnesium","It contains nickel and iron","It contains sodium and aluminium"],"correct":0,"explain":"SIAL = Si (silica) + Al (alumina) — the main mineral constituents of the continental mass."},{"q":["Consider the following pairs (Name — Composition):","I. SIAL — Silica and Alumina (continental crust)","II. SIMA — Silica and Magnesium (oceanic crust)","III. NIFE — Nickel and Ferrous/Iron (core)","How many of the above pairs are correctly matched?"],"options":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","All three pairs","None of the pairs"],"correct":2,"explain":"All three pairs are correctly matched — SIAL (continental crust), SIMA (oceanic crust), and NIFE (core) are each correctly paired with their compositions."},{"q":"The innermost layer of the Earth has a radius of about 3,500 km and is mainly made up of nickel and iron. It is called:","options":["SIAL","SIMA","NIFE","Mantle"],"correct":2,"explain":"NIFE (Ni = nickel, Fe = ferrous/iron) is the core — the innermost layer with a radius of about 3,500 km and very high temperature and pressure."},{"q":"What percentage of the Earth's total volume does the crust constitute?","options":["1%","15%","84%","50%"],"correct":0,"explain":"The crust forms only 1% of the volume of the Earth. The mantle constitutes 84% and the core 15%."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about the Earth's layers:","I. The Crust forms 1% of Earth's volume.","II. The Mantle constitutes 84% of Earth's volume.","III. The Core constitutes 84% of Earth's volume.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I and II only","I, II and III","I and III only","II and III only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Statements I and II are correct. Statement III is incorrect — the Core constitutes 15% (not 84%) of Earth's volume. The Mantle is the largest by volume at 84%."},{"q":"Rocks formed when molten magma cools and becomes solid are called:","options":["Sedimentary rocks","Metamorphic rocks","Igneous rocks","Primary rocks only"],"correct":2,"explain":"Igneous rocks (also called primary rocks) are formed when molten magma cools and solidifies. There are two types: extrusive (surface) and intrusive (underground)."},{"q":["Assertion (A): Basalt has a very fine grained structure.","Reason (R): Lava that reaches the Earth's surface cools rapidly, giving little time for crystals to grow, resulting in fine grained extrusive igneous rock.","Which one of the following is correct?"],"options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R correctly explains A — rapid cooling at the surface is precisely why extrusive rocks like basalt have fine grained structures."},{"q":"The Deccan Plateau is made up of which type of rock?","options":["Granite (intrusive igneous)","Basalt (extrusive igneous)","Marble (metamorphic)","Sandstone (sedimentary)"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Deccan Plateau is made up of basalt rocks — extrusive igneous rocks formed by rapid cooling of lava on the Earth's surface."},{"q":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes intrusive igneous rocks from extrusive igneous rocks?","options":["Intrusive: rapid cooling on surface → fine grained; Extrusive: slow cooling inside → large grains","Intrusive: slow cooling deep inside crust → large grains; Extrusive: rapid cooling on surface → fine grained","Both types cool at the same rate and have identical grain sizes","Intrusive rocks are formed from sediments; extrusive rocks from magma"],"correct":1,"explain":"Intrusive rocks (e.g. granite) cool SLOWLY deep inside → large grains. Extrusive rocks (e.g. basalt) cool RAPIDLY on the surface → fine grained."},{"q":"Sedimentary rocks may contain fossils. Fossils are best defined as:","options":["Minerals with definite chemical composition","Remains of dead plants and animals trapped in layers of rocks","Rocks formed under great heat and pressure","Molten material inside the Earth"],"correct":1,"explain":"Fossils are the remains of dead plants and animals (and other micro-organisms) that were trapped in the layers of rocks when the sediments were being compressed and hardened."},{"q":["Consider the following pairs (Rock — Example):","I. Extrusive igneous — Basalt","II. Sedimentary — Sandstone","III. Metamorphic — Granite","IV. Intrusive igneous — Granite","How many of the above pairs are correctly matched?"],"options":["Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs","Only one pair"],"correct":1,"explain":"Pairs I, II, and IV are correctly matched. Pair III is incorrect — Granite is an INTRUSIVE IGNEOUS rock, not a metamorphic rock."},{"q":"Which rock type is produced when igneous or sedimentary rocks are subjected to great heat and pressure?","options":["Another igneous rock","Sedimentary rock","Metamorphic rock","Fossil rock"],"correct":2,"explain":"Metamorphic rocks are formed when existing igneous or sedimentary rocks undergo great heat and pressure, changing their form. Example: Clay → Slate; Limestone → Marble."},{"q":["Consider the following transformations:","I. Clay → Slate","II. Limestone → Marble","III. Sandstone → Granite","Which of the above are correct examples of rock transformations into metamorphic rocks?"],"options":["I and II only","I, II and III","II and III only","I only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Transformations I and II are correct. Transformation III is incorrect — Sandstone does NOT change into Granite (Granite is an intrusive igneous rock). Sandstone can change into quartzite."},{"q":"The Red Fort in Delhi is made of which material?","options":["White marble","Red sandstone","Basalt","Granite"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Red Fort is made of RED SANDSTONE — a sedimentary rock. The Taj Mahal is made of white marble (metamorphic rock)."},{"q":"The Taj Mahal is made of which type of rock, and what category does it belong to?","options":["Red sandstone — sedimentary","Granite — intrusive igneous","White marble — metamorphic","Basalt — extrusive igneous"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Taj Mahal is made of white marble — a metamorphic rock (limestone transformed under heat and pressure)."},{"q":"The rock cycle is best described as:","options":["The cycle of rainfall that weathers rocks over time","The process by which one type of rock changes into another type in a cyclic manner through various geological processes","The process of human mining and use of rocks","The movement of tectonic plates carrying rocks"],"correct":1,"explain":"The rock cycle is the continuous process by which rocks are transformed from one type to another: Magma → Igneous → (breaks down) → Sedimentary → (heat/pressure) → Metamorphic → (melts) → Magma again."},{"q":"Which of the following correctly sequences the rock cycle?","options":["Magma → Igneous → Sediments → Sedimentary → Metamorphic → Magma","Sedimentary → Metamorphic → Igneous → Magma → Sediments","Metamorphic → Sedimentary → Igneous → Sediments → Magma","Magma → Metamorphic → Igneous → Sedimentary → Magma"],"correct":0,"explain":"The correct rock cycle sequence is: Magma cools → Igneous rocks → weathered/eroded → Sediments → compressed/hardened → Sedimentary rocks → heat and pressure → Metamorphic rocks → melted → Magma again."},{"q":["Consider the following statements:","I. The crust is thicker under ocean floors than under continents.","II. The mantle extends to a depth of 2,900 km below the crust.","III. Minerals like gold, coal and petroleum are examples of minerals important to humankind.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["II and III only","I and II only","I, II and III","III only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Statements II and III are correct. Statement I is incorrect — the OPPOSITE is true: the crust is ~35 km thick under continental masses but only ~5 km thick on ocean floors."}]}}});
